The Great Escape: the start positions drawn from the seed. "Referees/The Great Escape/Referee.java" of
https://github.com/SpiritusSancti5/codinGame (c0cc6fe) draws the players' start positions in initReferee with an
unseeded `new Random()` (four draws, nextInt(width) twice and nextInt(height) twice, used unless the init property
"positions" is given; more draws for a short list or a value out of range). The jar's CLI (referees/cg-builder/legacy)
passes only the init property "seed=N", so the jar built from the file as published plays other start positions on
every run: its games cannot be repeated, compared or replayed.

This patch seeds that Random with the init property "seed" (`new Random(Long.parseLong(seed))`); without a "seed"
property the referee draws unseeded, as published. Nothing else changes: the same draws in the same order, the same
use of "positions", "symmetric", "width" and "height", the same rules. Applied by legacy/build.sh for the output name
cg-2015-great-escape; there is no build of the file as published (it cannot be validated).

Why no other mapping: CodinGame's matches do not show a seed. Their replays (gameResult/findByGameId, 368 games of the
top league's players, 2 and 3 players) give the referee's getConfiguration() as the referee input: width=9, height=9,
positions (four values 0..8) and symmetric (true in 32 games), never a seed; each start configuration is played once
per seat permutation. So nothing ties CodinGame's positions to a seed: the seed here only makes the jar (and the C++
port, which draws the same positions from the same seed) deterministic. CodinGame's "symmetric=true" matches (player
1 on player 0's row, mirrored) are not played by the CLI (the referee's default is false).

